        <Literal xml:lang="en">data set</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:IAO_0000112"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:IAO_0000100</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Intensity values in a CEL file or from multiple CEL files comprise a data set (as opposed to the CEL files themselves).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:IAO_0000114"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:IAO_0000100</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:IAO_0000125</AbbreviatedIRI>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:IAO_0000115"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:IAO_0000100</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A data item that is an aggregate of other data items of the same type that have something in common. Averages and distributions can be determined for data sets.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:IAO_0000116"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:IAO_0000100</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">2009/10/23 Alan Ruttenberg. The intention is that this term represent collections of like data. So this isn&apos;t for, e.g. the whole contents of a cel file, which includes parameters, metadata etc. This is more like java arrays of a certain rather specific type</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="obo:IAO_0000116"/>
        <AbbreviatedIRI>obo:IAO_0000100</AbbreviatedIRI>
        <Literal>2014-05-05: Data sets are aggregates and thus must include two or more data items. We have chosen not to add logical axioms to make this restriction.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
